welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and today our  guest is Professor Susan Stokes she is  the John S Satan professor of political  science and director of the Yale program  on democracy professor Stokes research  interests include democratic theory and  how democracy functions in developing  societies with a focus on Latin America  her most recent book is democracy and  the culture of skepticism political  trust in Argentina and Mexico today we  will talk with Professor Stokes about  why leftist parties in Latin American  countries have recently won so much  electoral support welcome professor  Stokes thank you very much you recently  completed a paper on globalization and  the left in Latin America please give us  an overview and tell us some of your  findings well there are two notable  things that have happened recently in  Latin America one is that there's been a  big shift toward what's often called  neoliberalism meaning open markets  relatively open trade with the rest of  the world integration with capital  markets that's sort of on the economic  side and then on the political side  especially very recently there have been  victories by parties of the electoral  left Social Democratic parties sometimes  more left-wing parties and my paper  basically sort of puts those two facts  together it shows that the electoral  success of the Left is largely due or is  it is significantly due to parties at  the right coming to power and opening  trade and decreasing spending and  increasing capital market integration  and those changes tend to help the left  at the ballot box in the next election  okay let's talk a little bit about the  research for the paper I know you you  look at many different countries can you  give us a little overview on the  research that you've done well actually  my interest in a way in some of these  issues goes back to my dissertation  which I did in Peru and long time ago in  the 1980s I was interested in the fact  that uh  left-wing coalition had won elections at  the municipal level in in Lima which was  is a very big important city and so I've  actually been interested in electoral  politics in the left and and ideology  for a long time the current research  project really involved just sort of  keeping keeping tabs on what's been  going on I didn't actually spend you  know enormous amount of time flying  around the region talking to leaders  it's more it's a large statistical cross  national study informed by my own  expertise in Latin America which I've  been studying for ever since I finished  graduate school I was in graduate school  okay so in the countries you've looked  at what is the effect of globalization  and state size on elections in those  latin-american countries well it tends  to help the left in elections especially  when the when rightist parties or  right-leaning parties come into office  and and open up capital markets open up  markets to goods and services and  decrease the size of the state that is  have less government spending that tends  to help the left on average in in the  next election you can see a little bit  of the same pattern may be happening in  the United States in our recent  electoral cycle where a big issue on the  Democratic side was NAFTA and free trade  and and redistributed policies that  tended to concentrate wealth that  probably helped the Democrats and  similar kind of thing is was has been  true in Latin American elections okay so  what is the effect of the left in power  on globalization and on spending well  that's the other side of the story so  part of the story is what happens in  elections when these economic changes  happen and then the second part of the  story is what happens to the economic  changes when the left comes to power and  there the interesting story is that  rather even though the left has  benefited electorally from opening of  trade and and capital markets under  right-wing administrations it doesn't  then once in office  reduce globalization reduce the exposure  of the of the country to international  markets rather it compensates for that  exposure by increasing spending my own  research indicates that the left in  office increases general social spending  and then there are other studies that  suggest that particularly spending on  health and human capital education  increase under the left in in the region  one of the things you touch on in your  paper is an anti biased towards trade  for some of the candidates can you  expound upon that again yeah well that's  the interesting thing they that they are  they've been careful not to promise to  you know cut free-trade agreements or to  increase protectionist tariffs they're  not the candidates in the electoral  campaigns especially the left-leaning  candidates don't go out of their way to  make those kinds of promises but they do  in more couched language suggest that  they'll revise aspects of the Nationals  of the North American Free Trade  Agreement for example and especially in  the Mexican case again very much like  the Obama Clinton debate during the  campaign here it's a matter of sort of  suggesting that globalization is not  entirely good for especially for their  constituents but then once in office I  think there are cross-cutting pressures  that tend to discourage these  left-of-center presidents now from  really closing down on trade it would  hurt some others of their constituents I  think there's a lot of uncertainty about  what the effects of those policies would  be so so instead of doing that they try  to increase spending as a way of sort of  compensating those who are maybe the  losers or who experience more volatility  when when globalization advances in  doing the research was there anything  that particularly surprised you I think  that what surprised me was the degree to  which the Latin American cases were  actually similar to two cases that we  know in the advanced industrial  countries so I went into the project  thinking that believing that  notion that been put out there by other  scholars that there was a dramatic kind  of exceptionalism going on in Latin  America the general finding in the  advanced democracies has been that the  more open a country is to trade on  average the larger the government that  public sector will be and again the the  reason the rationale is that you need a  large public sector to cushion the  effects of globalization it looked for a  long time especially going back to the  1990s as though the Latin American  countries were going in the opposite  direction they were increasing trade and  at the same time cutting very  drastically sometimes cutting that the  the state cutting the public sector the  more I looked into it the more it looked  like it's sort of a story that's not all  that unlike what happened in what  happens in European democracies perhaps  the United States and other advanced  democracies wealthier countries where  the you get an increase in trade that  leads to an increase of social spent of  social spending in general government  spending but only under the conditions  that a left-of-centre or social  democratic kind of government comes to  power but those conditions do tend to  make it more likely that such a  government will come to power so really  the story doesn't look so different from  the one that we're more familiar with in  the advanced democracies the difference  being that the state sector probably  doesn't function as well in Latin  America as an insurance policy against  volatility or as a Redistributor of  resources from the losers from trade to  the wind but from the winners to trade  to the losers I should say ok well  thanks very much for being with us today  and sharing some of your research thank  you very much for more information about  professor Stokes and her research please  visit our website at yale.edu backslash  macmillan report be sure to join us  again for another episode of the  Macmillan report made possible through  funding from the Whitney and Betty  Macmillan Center for International and  area studies at Yale
 

